  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 PM MDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nThe second surge of moisture has largely come to an end and thus we \nhave cancelled the Flood Watch as flood risk has ceased. Light \nprecipitation will continue to fall through late Tuesday, \npredominantly across the south hills, southeast highlands, and \nportions of the central mountains, where roughly another 0.25\" to \nhalf an inch of additional liquid accumulation is forecast over the \nnext 24 hours. The highest elevations of the central mountains and \nsouth hills will likely receive localized amounts of 1\" to 4\" inches \nof snow accumulation.\n\nWe will continue Frost/Freeze headlines for the next couple of \nmornings. While National Blend probabilities do not show strong \nsupport for widespread sub-freezing temperatures, area observations \nindicate widespread temperatures dropped into sub-freezing range \nacross our growing zones south and west of Idaho Falls. That\nsaid, cloud cover will continue to be a limiting factor \ntonight/early Tuesday morning. Any sub-freezing temperature will\nlikely be short- lived before a quick return to the low to \nmid-30s. Once clouds clear, we anticipate widespread sub-\nfreezing temperatures Wednesday morning with an end to the \ngrowing season also expected. \n\nWhile early morning low temperatures will run coldest Wednesday \nmorning, daytime temperatures will continue to warm through the \nduration of the extended period as high pressure builds over the \nregion from the west. Temperatures today will start out around 15 \ndegrees below normal with widespread 40s, warming to several degrees \nabove normal into the 60s by early next week.",
